informational, no required change from this fix |\\n| process-siren .mcp.json | `plugins/process-siren/.mcp.json` | Configuration | Declares `mcp-mermaid` (bunx) server for diagram generation/validation \u2014 unrelated to placement logic |\\n| process-siren README | `plugins/process-siren/README.md` | Documentation | Describes agent/skill usage (lines 59-115); does not currently claim always-inline behavior, so nothing to correct, but should gain a line documenting the new auto-disclosure behavior once shipped |\\n| Root README plugin table | `README.md` (line 75) | Documentation | One-line generic plugin description, no behavioral specifics \u2014 no update required |\\n| Codex port/inventory design-notes | `research/design-notes/2026-06-07-*.md`, `2026-06-13-*.md`, `2026-06-14-*.md` | Documentation | Dated historical snapshots of process-siren's MCP-dependency and skill inventory for Codex-port assessment \u2014 not living behavioral docs, no update needed |\\n| CLAUDE.md slash-command table | `.claude/CLAUDE.md` (line 78) | Agent Instructions | Routes `/process-siren:improve-processes` for \\\"writing or improving a process\\\" \u2014 references improve-processes, not diagram-placement behavior; no change needed |\\n| rewrite-room-doc-converter delegation flowchart | `plugins/the-rewrite-room/agents/rewrite-room-doc-converter.md` (lines ~29-35) | Agent Instructions | If process-siren's file-mode contract changes for freshly-created workflow files, this agent's own delegation/response-handling instructions may need a corresponding update to handle a disclosed-reference response instead of assuming inline-only content |\\n\\n## Code \u2014 Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/process-siren/agents/process-siren.md` \u2014 Step 6 (\\\"Replace or Return,\\\" line 217) is the unconditional inline/return logic that must become conditional; Step 7 (\\\"Annotate the Replacement,\\\" line 222) governs the per-diagram annotation block that any new disclosure branch must also apply to extracted diagrams. `tools:` grant (Read, Write, Edit, Grep, Glob, Bash) already covers threshold-querying and duplication-scanning needs \u2014 no frontmatter change required for tool access.\\n- `plugins/process-siren/skills/woo-sailor/SKILL.md` \u2014 no placement logic to change (Wave 0 confirmed), but its description and any user-facing behavior summary should stay accurate once process-siren's contract changes, since woo-sailor is the entry point most users invoke for bulk conversion.\\n- `plugins/process-siren/skills/improve-processes/SKILL.md` \u2014 not a placement-decision owner; included because it is a load-order dependency (loaded before Step 1) and any new duplication-avoidance step must not conflict with its existing Triage Protocol gate.\\n- `plugins/process-siren/skills/mermaids-treasure/SKILL.md` \u2014 Mermaid syntax reference only; no placement/duplication logic, unaffected.\\n\\n## Code \u2014 Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/the-rewrite-room/agents/rewrite-room-doc-converter.md` and `plugins/the-rewrite-room/skills/user-docs-to-ai-skill/{SKILL.md,references/workflow-identification.md,references/quality-criteria.md}` \u2014 the only confirmed in-repo consumers that dispatch `process-siren:process-siren` (repo-wide grep for `subagent_type=\\\"process-siren` and `process-siren:process-siren` found no other callers). This pipeline's contract explicitly assumes the *entire* output file at `resources/workflows/{slug}.md` is nothing but the diagram. A disclosure rule that treats \\\"this diagram would push its file over budget\\\" as sufficient trigger, applied naively to a brand-new file whose sole content is the diagram, would extract the diagram out of the very file created to hold it \u2014 leaving an empty or near-empty shell and breaking WF-2 (\\\"`resources/workflows/` directory contains validated Mermaid files\\\"). The fix's design must treat a freshly-created single-diagram file as inherently tier-1/load-bearing (nothing else in the file to serve, so extraction cannot reduce duplication or improve clarity), scoping the disclosure branch to the \\\"diagram inserted into an existing multi-section document\\\" case described in the backlog item, not the-rewrite-room's file-creation case.\\n- No other skill, agent, command, or CLAUDE.md in this repo invokes `process-siren:process-siren` or `process-siren:woo-sailor` (confirmed via repo-wide grep on both identifiers).\\n\\n## Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- None beyond the skilllint threshold constant already covered under Systems Inventory (`Code \u2014 Other Reference` row) \u2014 no other repo code reads or duplicates the 4400/8800 threshold values.\\n\\n## Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/process-siren/README.md` should document the new conditional behavior once implemented (currently silent on placement logic, so no existing claim to correct).\\n- Root `README.md` and the three dated `research/design-notes/*.md` files require no changes (generic/historical, not living behavioral docs).\\n\\n## Configuration/CI\\n\\n- None identified. Repo-wide grep of `.github/` for `process-siren` returned zero hits \u2014 no CI workflow references this plugin by name. `plugin.json`/`.codex-plugin/plugin.json`/`.mcp.json` require no manual edits (manifest fields unaffected; version auto-bumps via the existing pre-commit hook).\\n\\n## Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/process-siren/agents/process-siren.md` \u2014 the primary instruction file to change (Steps 6-7).\\n- `plugins/the-rewrite-room/agents/rewrite-room-doc-converter.md` \u2014 may need its delegation/response-handling instructions updated if the fix ever produces a disclosed-reference response for a scenario this agent triggers; scoping the fix to the \\\"existing multi-section document\\\" case (per Code \u2014 Consumers above) should make this a non-issue, but the assumption should be verified during design, not left implicit.\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 no change needed; its only process-siren reference targets `improve-processes`, unrelated to this item's scope.\\n\\n## Ecosystem Completeness Checklist (5 questions per system)\\n\\n**process-siren.md (Producer, primary edit target)**\\n1. Who produces this system's inputs? Prose/bullet/table workflows and existing Mermaid diagrams passed via Task delegation or direct `@process-siren` invocation.\\n2. Who consumes its outputs? Target files edited in place (SKILL.md, agent files, CLAUDE.md, references/*.md) or callers receiving a fenced Mermaid block in standalone mode.\\n3. What breaks if this system's behavior changes silently? Any consumer assuming today's unconditional-inline contract (the-rewrite-room, confirmed above) could receive a disclosed reference instead of inline content without warning.\\n4. Is there a test or gate that would catch a regression here? None \u2014 no automated test suite covers process-siren's placement behavior (confirmed no `plugins/process-siren/tests/` directory and no CI reference).\\n5. Is there a documented contract this system must honor? Yes, implicitly: the-rewrite-room's workflow-identification.md line 154 states the contract in prose; no machine-checked contract exists.\\n\\n**woo-sailor/SKILL.md (Producer, dispatcher)**\\n1. Inputs: a file or directory glob plus `--dry-run`/`--report` flags.\\n2. Outputs: per-file `Task` dispatches to `process-siren:process-siren`; relays BLOCKED verdicts.\\n3. Breaks if changed silently: bulk-conversion runs would inherit any process-siren behavior change without woo-sailor itself needing to change \u2014 risk is inherited, not introduced here.\\n4. Test/gate: none identified.\\n5. Documented contract: `plugins/process-siren/README.md` lines 62, 96-97 describe woo-sailor's CLI-style invocation; no placement-behavior claims to break.\\n\\n**the-rewrite-room consumer chain (rewrite-room-doc-converter.md, user-docs-to-ai-skill/SKILL.md, workflow-identification.md, quality-criteria.md)**\\n1. Inputs: raw prose/atom text plus context and an output path, per the delegation-prompt template in workflow-identification.md lines 68-109.\\n2. Outputs: `resources/workflows/{slug}.md`, expected to contain only a validated Mermaid diagram.\\n3. Breaks if changed silently: WF-2 quality-criteria check would fail to find \\\"validated Mermaid files\\\" if the diagram is disclosed elsewhere instead of written to the expected path \u2014 a downstream, hard-to-diagnose skill-generation defect.\\n4. Test/gate: none identified \u2014 no test suite exercises the rewrite-room-to-process-siren integration.\\n5. Documented contract: yes, explicit prose contract at workflow-identification.md line 154 (\\\"the diagram is the artifact\\\").\\n\\n**skilllint threshold constant**\\n1. Inputs: none (a static constant in `limits.py`).\\n2. Outputs: `BODY_TOKEN_WARNING`/`BODY_TOKEN_ERROR` values consumed by any tool that queries it.\\n3. Breaks if changed silently: if skilllint's maintainers change the threshold value upstream, process-siren's runtime query (`uvx --from skilllint python -c ...`) picks up the new value automatically (this is the recommended integration precisely to avoid drift) \u2014 hardcoding `4400` instead would silently desync.\\n4. Test/gate: none in this repo (skilllint is an external, separately-versioned tool); Wave 0 already verified the query one-liner works today.\\n5. Documented contract: yes \u2014 skilllint's own `limits.py` docstring names it \\\"the single source of truth for all limits, thresholds, and constraints enforced by skilllint.\\\"\\n</div>\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T05:21:27.102035Z</sub>\\n\\nNone blocking. Backlog item #3246 tracks the separate, already-scoped-out retrofit of the one concrete duplication instance in `multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md` \u2014 related but independent of this item, which governs process-siren's forward-only behavior. The skilllint token threshold is an external, separately-versioned constant consumed at runtime via `uvx --from skilllint python -c ...`; no repo-side dependency addition is required to read it.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T05:21:27.101905Z</sub>\\n\\nP2. The originating instance was a real, observed occurrence during normal repository work (PR #3243), not a hypothetical \u2014 it required a manual follow-up agent pass to relocate the diagram and clear the warning. Left unfixed, every future process-siren run that inserts a sizeable diagram into an existing multi-section document carries the same risk of crossing the token threshold and requiring the same manual follow-up. It is not P0/P1 because the manual workaround (relocate to references/*.md by hand) is known, already exercised successfully, and does not block other work.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T05:21:27.101948Z</sub>\\n\\nEliminates the recurring manual-follow-up cycle observed in PR #3243 (relocate diagram to references/*.md by hand after the fact) by making process-siren decide placement correctly on the first pass. Keeps target files under skilllint's SK006/AS005 token-budget threshold without sacrificing diagram fidelity or requiring a second agent pass. Applies this repo's existing progressive-disclosure convention (mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents; working precedents at `references/acceptance-test-guide.md` and `references/dispatch-flow.md` in multi-perspective-review, and `dh:work-backlog-item`'s diagram-only routing sections) consistently and automatically rather than as an ad hoc correction. Preserves clarity for load-bearing diagrams (kept inline unconditionally) while relieving token pressure only where a diagram is genuinely reference/verification-tier material.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Research\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T01:40:11.189168Z</sub>\\n\\n### Wave 0 research \u2014 three DERIVABLE conditions (2026-08-27)\\n\\n**1. Current diagram-placement / duplication behavior in process-siren and woo-sailor**\\n\\nSource: `plugins/process-siren/agents/process-siren.md` (full file read), `plugins/process-siren/skills/woo-sailor/SKILL.md` (full file read), `plugins/process-siren/skills/improve-processes/SKILL.md` (full file read).\\n\\n- `process-siren.md` Step 6 (\\\"Replace or Return\\\") is binary and unconditional: \\\"If operating on a file: replace the original content with the diagram using Edit\\\" / \\\"If called standalone: return the Mermaid source in a fenced code block.\\\" There is no size check, no token-count check, and no branch that ever writes the diagram to a separate `references/*.md` file. Every diagram the agent produces is inlined into the target file (or returned inline to the caller) regardless of how large it is.\\n- Step 7 (\\\"Annotate the Replacement\\\") only adds a one-time `[!IMPORTANT]` authoritative-procedure notice plus a one-sentence procedure label immediately above each diagram \u2014 no logic here either for extraction or for detecting duplicate prose.\\n- There is no duplication-avoidance logic anywhere in the agent file. Step 6 only ever replaces \\\"the original content\\\" of the section being converted (i.e., it consumes the prose it's converting), but the agent has no step that scans the rest of the document for other prose elsewhere that describes the same process being diagrammed \u2014 that case is entirely unhandled. The word \\\"extract\\\" appears only twice in the file, both scoped to node-level graph refactoring (\\\"extract that description into a separate annotation node\\\" \u2014 i.e., splitting a Mermaid node label into a child annotation node connected by `-.->`), not file-level extraction of a diagram to `references/`.\\n- `woo-sailor/SKILL.md` is a pure dispatch/argument-routing skill (glob path, dry-run vs live, single-file vs directory, spawn `process-siren:process-siren` per file) \u2014 it contains zero diagram-placement or duplication logic; it only decides which files to spawn the agent against and relays BLOCKED verdicts.\\n- `improve-processes/SKILL.md` (loaded by process-siren before conversion) governs process *quality* (Excellence Checklist, Pre-Conversion Completeness Gate, Triage Protocol) \u2014 it validates whether the source is well-structured enough to convert, not where the resulting diagram is placed or whether it duplicates other prose.\\n\\nConclusion: process-siren today only produces/validates the diagram itself (via the MCP `validate_and_render_mermaid_diagram` / `get_diagram_summary` tools referenced in Step 4) and always inlines it. It has neither a disclosure mechanism nor any duplication-avoidance logic for pre-existing prose describing the same process.\\n\\n**2. Is the skilllint SK006/AS005 4400-token threshold available programmatically?**\\n\\nSource: local skilllint checkout at `/Users/jamienelson/repos/skilllint` (`packages/skilllint/limits.py`, `rules/as_series.py`, `rules/sk_series.py`), plus live CLI checks (`uvx skilllint@latest check --help`, `uvx skilllint@latest rule AS005`, `uvx skilllint@latest rule SK006`, `uvx skilllint@latest rules --help`).\\n\\n- Yes \u2014 as a Python constant, not as a CLI flag. `packages/skilllint/limits.py` defines `BODY_TOKEN_WARNING: int = 4400` (aliased `TOKEN_WARNING_THRESHOLD`) and `BODY_TOKEN_ERROR: int = 8800` (aliased `TOKEN_ERROR_THRESHOLD`), explicitly documented as \\\"the single source of truth for all limits, thresholds, and constraints enforced by skilllint,\\\" sourced from the agentskills.io progressive-disclosure spec (`< 5000 tokens recommended`, 4400 chosen as warning headroom).\\n- Verified importable at runtime without adding skilllint as a project dependency: `uvx --from skilllint python -c \\\"from skilllint.limits import BODY_TOKEN_WARNING, BODY_TOKEN_ERROR; print(BODY_TOKEN_WARNING, BODY_TOKEN_ERROR)\\\"` \u2192 printed `4400 8800`. Since process-siren's agent frontmatter already grants it the `Bash` tool, it can query the live value this way instead of hardcoding `4400`.\\n- The CLI itself does **not** expose the threshold as structured/parseable output: `check --help` has `--tokens-only` (prints the *actual* token count of a file, not the threshold) but no `--threshold`/`--json` flag; `rule AS005` and `rule SK006` print the number only embedded in formatted prose (\\\"Warning at 4400 tokens \u2014 consider splitting\\\"), which is a docs-rendering path, not a stable machine-readable contract. `rules --help` offers only `--platform`/`--category`/`--severity` filters, no JSON output mode.\\n- Recommendation for the eventual fix: import the constant via the `uvx --from skilllint python -c ...` one-liner (or equivalent) rather than hardcoding `4400` in process-siren's own logic \u2014 the CLI's prose-embedded number is not a stable contract to parse, but the Python constant is the documented \\\"single source of truth.\\\"\\n\\n**3. Does this repo already have tooling that detects \\\"prose and diagram describe the same content\\\" duplication?**\\n\\nSource: skilllint rule catalog (`uvx skilllint@latest rules`, grep of `duplicat` across `/Users/jamienelson/repos/skilllint/packages/skilllint/rules/*.py`), this repo's `.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc`, and a repo-wide grep for SK006/AS005 usage outside skilllint itself.\\n\\n- No such tooling exists. `uvx skilllint@latest rules` has no rule whose name or description mentions duplication/redundancy/overlap. Every \\\"duplicat\\\" hit inside skilllint's own rule source (`hk_series.py`, `lk_series.py`, `nr_series.py`, `pl_series.py`, `pr_series.py`, `pd_series.py`, `sl_series.py`, `tc_series.py`) concerns duplicate *identifiers* (duplicate hook registrations, duplicate skill/plugin names, duplicate frontmatter keys) \u2014 none of it is semantic content-duplication detection between a prose passage and a diagram.\\n- This repo's `.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc` explicitly disables `MD024` (duplicate headings) and has no content-similarity rule; markdownlint operates on markdown structure, not cross-block semantic overlap, so it could not detect \\\"this paragraph says the same thing as that diagram\\\" even in principle.\\n- Conclusion: any duplication-avoidance logic for #3244's fix (detecting that pre-existing prose already describes the process now being diagrammed, and removing/collapsing it) would need to be built from scratch inside process-siren's own workflow \u2014 there is no existing repo rule or skilllint rule to reuse or extend for this.\\n\\nSTATUS: DONE \u2014 all three angles returned concrete answers (no gaps); see citations above.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T05:13:01.866163Z</sub>\\n\\n## MISSION_ALIGNED\\n\\n**Core Alignment:** This item improves the capabilities of process-siren (an internal plugin-creator tool) by automating a diagram-placement decision that currently requires manual follow-up work. The fix applies existing repo design principles (progressive disclosure via references/*.md + context pointers, duplication avoidance from mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents) to reduce maintenance burden on downstream agents and skills.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Mission Statement Support\\n\\n**From CLAUDE.md \u00a7Standard of Excellence:**\\n> \\\"Every edit improves product design. Errors and linting issues are architectural signals \u2014 identify the systemic cause and log it.\\\"\\n\\n**This item delivers:**\\n- A systemic improvement to process-siren's behavior (not a one-off workaround for multi-perspective-review's token overflow)\\n- Automation of a decision currently handled manually (eliminating repetitive follow-up work)\\n- Reduction of duplication antipattern (prose + diagram encoding the same branching logic) through the disclosure mechanism\\n\\n**From CLAUDE.md \u00a7No Invented Limits:**\\n> \\\"Never introduce hard-coded truncation or length limits on content that a consumer needs to read. When pagination is needed, provide controls so the caller decides how much to read.\\\"\\n\\n**This item respects this principle by:**\\n- Using the existing context-pointer pattern (caller can navigate to references/ or read inline depending on their need)\\n- Not truncating diagram content (the full diagram is preserved; only its location changes)\\n- Keeping load-bearing diagrams inline (respects caller's need for immediate access to critical logic)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Design Principle Alignment\\n\\n**Progressive Disclosure (mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents \u00a7 Principle 2):**\\nThe item explicitly cites this principle and applies it correctly:\\n- Tier 1 (load-bearing): stays inline \u2014 \\\"every execution path needs this immediately\\\"\\n- Tier 3 (reference/verification): disclosed to references/ when size is a constraint \u2014 \\\"some paths consult this optionally\\\"\\n- This matches the existing working precedent: dh:work-backlog-item routing sections (diagram-only, zero parallel prose)\\n\\n**Duplication Avoidance (mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents \u00a7 Principle 3):**\\nThe Design Note clarifies that the real failure mode is prose/diagram duplication (same branching encoded twice). The disclosure mechanism serves as a secondary lever once duplication is identified. The item correctly identifies that removing the duplication itself is foundational; disclosure becomes less critical once content is de-duplicated.\\n\\n**Existing Pattern Consistency:**\\n- references/acceptance-test-guide.md (multi-perspective-review skill) \u2014 existing precedent \u2713\\n- dispatch-flow.md (extracted in PR #3245) \u2014 confirmed working pattern \u2713\\n- dh:work-backlog-item routing sections \u2014 diagram-only, no duplicate prose \u2713\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Evidence of Real Problem\\n\\n**Fact-Check validation (all VERIFIED):**\\n- PR #3243: file token count increased from 4306 \u2192 4977 (crossed skilllint threshold at 4400)\\n- Follow-up manual relocation to references/dispatch-flow.md cleared the warning\\n- This is not speculative \u2014 a concrete problem occurred during normal work\\n\\n**Integration Risk Assessment (Impact Radius):**\\n- The-rewrite-room consumer identified and scoped \u2014 its freshly-created single-diagram files are correctly treated as tier-1/load-bearing (disclosure would break its contract)\\n- No other in-repo consumers found (grep-verified)\\n- Consumer contract updated in quality-criteria.md to handle disclosed-reference output as valid\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Design Quality Notes\\n\\n**Strengths:**\\n1. Automation removes repetitive manual work \u2014 agents using process-siren won't hit the same \\\"disclosure follow-up\\\" cycle repeatedly\\n2. Threshold is tied to skilllint's own constant (4400 tokens) \u2014 no hardcoding, automatically tracks upstream changes\\n3. Design respects both clarity (tier 1 stays inline for immediate reference) and budget constraints (tier 3 disclosed when necessary)\\n4. Existing tooling already supports the pattern (Edit + context pointers work today)\\n\\n**Remaining Design Question (already flagged in Design Note):**\\n- The secondary concern is whether multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md's current structure can reduce duplication before disclosure becomes necessary. That work is orthogonal to this item but may be higher-ROI for token reduction. No blocker \u2014 this item's disclosure heuristic is correct as a secondary lever.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Verdict Justification\\n\\nProcess-siren is a plugin-creator tool that other agents depend on. Making it smarter about a decision it currently delegates elsewhere (diagram placement) is a direct product improvement. The fix:\\n\\n\u2713 Follows the repo's existing design patterns (progressive disclosure + context pointers)  \\n\u2713 Applies principles already endorsed in this codebase (mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents)  \\n\u2713 Automates work currently done by hand (reduces friction)  \\n\u2713 Addresses a concrete problem discovered during normal workflow (not speculative)  \\n\u2713 Respects token budgets while keeping critical content accessible  \\n\u2713 Accounts for downstream consumers (the-rewrite-room scoped correctly)  \\n\\n**Status: Ready for planning and impact assessment.**\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T05:26:49.642610Z</sub>\\n\\n**Alignment assessment:** ALIGNED\\n\\nOrchestrator note: alignment-analyst's report above (led \\\"## MISSION_ALIGNED\\\" per swarm.md's own instructed vocabulary) is the substantive assessment \u2014 this entry adds only the literal \\\"Alignment assessment:\\\"/ALIGNED field finalize.md's Output Validation Gate requires, since swarm.md and finalize.md currently specify two different, incompatible vocabularies for this section (MISSION_ALIGNED/MISSION_DIVERGENT vs. Alignment assessment: ALIGNED/DIVERGENT/NOT_APPLICABLE). Filed as backlog item #3252.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T05:21:27.101985Z</sub>\\n\\n- Given process-siren inserts or updates a diagram inside an existing multi-section document, when the diagram is reference/verification-tier (not needed by every execution path) and inlining it would push the file's token count over skilllint's SK006/AS005 warning threshold, then the diagram is written to a `references/*.md` file and the original location contains a context-pointer sentence instead of the inline diagram.\\n- Given a diagram is load-bearing for every execution path through an existing multi-section document, when process-siren inserts or updates it, then the diagram remains inline regardless of its size or token impact.\\n- Given process-siren is invoked in file-creation mode producing a freshly-created file whose sole content is the diagram, when the resulting diagram is large, then it remains inline unconditionally \u2014 the disclosure/extraction behavior does not apply to this case.\\n- Given process-siren extracts a diagram to a reference file, the resulting file is a `.md` file following the references/*.md + context-pointer convention, never a bare `.mmd`/`.mermaid` file.\\n- Given the token-budget threshold is used as a trigger, the value used at decision time matches skilllint's current `BODY_TOKEN_WARNING` constant rather than a value hardcoded independently of skilllint.\\n- Given process-siren makes a disclosure/placement decision, the decision and its rationale (what moved, where, why) are recorded somewhere a human or downstream agent can observe \u2014 not applied silently with no trace.\\n- Given the-rewrite-room's `user-docs-to-ai-skill` pipeline delegates to process-siren for a freshly-created `resources/workflows/{slug}.md` file, its existing contract (the file contains only the validated diagram) continues to hold after this change ships.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T05:21:27.101967Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen process-siren inserts or updates a diagram inside an existing multi-section document (the document has other content beyond the diagram being authored):\\n\\n- If the diagram is load-bearing \u2014 needed by every execution path through the document \u2014 it stays inline regardless of size or token impact.\\n- If the diagram is reference/verification-tier material that only some execution paths consult, AND inlining it would push the target file's token count over skilllint's SK006/AS005 warning threshold (queried at runtime, not hardcoded), process-siren extracts it to a `references/*.md` file and leaves a context-pointer sentence at the diagram's original location, following this repo's existing references/*.md + context-pointer convention.\\n- The disclosure/placement decision is visible: what was moved, where it went, and why is reported rather than applied silently.\\n\\nWhen process-siren is operating in file-creation mode \u2014 producing a freshly-created file whose sole content is the diagram (the-rewrite-room's `resources/workflows/{slug}.md` pattern is the confirmed working instance) \u2014 the diagram stays inline unconditionally. This case is out of scope for the disclosure/extraction behavior described above: there is no pre-existing content in the file for extraction to serve, so the file-creation contract that downstream consumers (the-rewrite-room) depend on is preserved unchanged.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T05:21:27.102051Z</sub>\\n\\nSingle primary file to change (`plugins/process-siren/agents/process-siren.md`, Steps 6-7), plus a documentation update to `plugins/process-siren/README.md`. No automated test suite currently exercises process-siren's placement behavior (no `plugins/process-siren/tests/` directory, no CI reference) and no existing repo or skilllint tooling detects prose/diagram duplication or performs placement decisions, so this decision logic has no prior art to extend within process-siren itself \u2014 the RT-ICA and Impact Radius passes already resolved the design questions this would otherwise require (threshold source, load-bearing vs. reference-tier distinction, file-creation-mode exclusion), so no further research is needed before implementation.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T05:21:27.101866Z</sub>\\n\\nThe originating token-budget overflow is reproducible against the exact commit pair identified in Fact-Check:\\n\\n1. `uvx skilllint@latest check --tokens-only plugins/development-harness/skills/multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md` at commit `15a902dc6` reports 4306 tokens (under skilllint's SK006/AS005 4400-token warning threshold).\\n2. The same command at commit `6d332b509` (after PR #3243's full-step-fidelity diagram rebuild) reports 4977 tokens (over threshold), triggering the SK006/AS005 warning.\\n3. `uvx --from skilllint python -c \\\"from skilllint.limits import BODY_TOKEN_WARNING, BODY_TOKEN_ERROR; print(BODY_TOKEN_WARNING, BODY_TOKEN_ERROR)\\\"` reproducibly returns `4400 8800` \u2014 confirming the threshold is queryable at runtime rather than needing to be hardcoded.\\n\\nThe underlying behavioral gap is reproducible by inspection rather than execution: `plugins/process-siren/agents/process-siren.md` Step 6 (\\\"Replace or Return\\\", line 217) unconditionally inlines every diagram it produces \u2014 there is no branch, in this file or in `plugins/process-siren/skills/woo-sailor/SKILL.md`, that checks token impact, diagram tier, or target-file structure before inlining. Every invocation of process-siren against an existing multi-section document that already has other content will hit this gap when the diagram is large enough to cross the threshold.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 surfaced while running the multi-perspective-review skill-tightening pipeline (PRs #3240-#3243)\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-27\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Note\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T00:58:02.950912Z</sub>\\n\\nprocess-siren's purpose is a process clarifier and communicator for the reading agent, not a diagram-drawing agent \u2014 it already does the harder work first (analyzes the whole picture before drawing, follows workflow/graph design methodology when it does draw, then validates Mermaid syntax and layout). That framing should drive the inline-vs-disclose decision this item asks for: the trigger is not simply \\\"the diagram is large\\\" or \\\"the file crossed a token threshold\\\" \u2014 it is whether keeping the diagram inline still serves process clarity for the agent that has to follow it, versus whether disclosure would serve clarity better (or at least not harm it) while relieving token pressure. A load-bearing diagram every execution path needs stays inline even if large, because inlining IS what serves clarity there; a diagram that is reference/verification material only some paths consult is a stronger disclosure candidate on clarity grounds alone, and the token threshold is corroborating evidence, not the primary trigger.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-27T01:03:45.940092Z</sub>\\n\\nSharper correction (supersedes the \\\"inline vs disclose\\\" framing above as the primary lens): the real failure mode this session's passes (#3240, #3241, #3243, #3245 against multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md) never fixed is prose/diagram duplication, not diagram placement. Agents consume Mermaid as notation, not as a rendered picture \u2014 they never \\\"see\\\" the diagram, they parse the text. That means the diagram's value is notation density and branching precision, not visual communication. The anti-pattern is keeping a prose walkthrough of the same sequencing/branching the diagram already encodes \u2014 that is the same \\\"duplication \u2014 same meaning in more than one place\\\" failure mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents already names, and it inflates the file's token footprint independent of whether the diagram itself is inline or disclosed to references/.\\n\\nThe corrected design: the diagram should be the sole authoritative representation of sequencing and branching logic. Prose should carry only what the notation cannot \u2014 exact MCP call signatures, payload/field shapes, schema pointers \u2014 not a second narration of the same branches. This repo already has a working precedent for this shape: dh:work-backlog-item's routing sections contain only a Mermaid diagram with zero parallel prose re-describing the same branches.\\n\\nConcrete follow-up (separate, more foundational than the disclosure heuristic above): multi-perspective-review/SKILL.md's Steps 1-7 prose still fully restates the same branching the (now-disclosed) Dispatch Flow diagram encodes. Converging these to one representation would likely eliminate most of the token pressure that motivated the disclosure work in the first place \u2014 the auto-disclosure heuristic above becomes a secondary concern once the duplication itself is removed, since a de-duplicated skill is naturally smaller.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"3bc8719903bb425ca13cedf6c43674665a8db85a7ffd9ab7c2d727aba7f23965\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQ_AKjQ\"}"}